# 11 — Prototypes & Prototypal Inheritance

## What is this?
JavaScript uses prototypal inheritance rather than classical inheritance. Every JavaScript object has an internal link to another object called its prototype (`[[Prototype]]`). When a property is accessed on an object, JavaScript searches the object itself, and if not found, traverses up the prototype chain until it reaches `Object.prototype` (or `null`).

## Why does JavaScript have it?
- Prototypes allow methods and shared properties to exist once in memory while being shared across millions of object instances.

## Basic Syntax
```javascript
const animal = {
    isAlive: true,
    breathe() {
        return "Breathing";
    }
};

// Create object inheriting from animal
const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.bark = function() {
    return "Woof!";
};

console.log(dog.breathe()); // "Breathing" (delegated to animal prototype)
```

## How it Behaves
1. `Object.create(proto)`: Creates a new object with its internal `[[Prototype]]` set to `proto`.
2. Property Shadowing: If you assign a property directly to a child object (`dog.isAlive = false`), it creates an "own property" on `dog`, masking the prototype's property without modifying the prototype.
3. `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)`: Recommended standard way to retrieve an object's prototype.
4. `Object.hasOwn(obj, prop)`: Returns `true` only if `prop` is directly defined on `obj`, not inherited via prototype.

## Common Mistakes
1. Using deprecated `obj.__proto__`: Use `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)` and `Object.setPrototypeOf(obj)` instead.
2. Modifying built-in prototypes: Never add methods to `Array.prototype` or `Object.prototype` (known as "monkey patching"), as it breaks libraries and causes collision bugs.

## Small Practical Example
```javascript
function createVehicle(make, model) {
    const proto = {
        describe() {
            return `${this.make} ${this.model}`;
        }
    };
    const vehicle = Object.create(proto);
    vehicle.make = make;
    vehicle.model = model;
    return vehicle;
}
```

## What to Practice
