# 13 — Error Handling & Custom Errors

## What is this?
Error handling allows programs to intercept and recover from runtime failures gracefully without crashing. JavaScript uses `try`, `catch`, `finally`, and `throw`. Custom error classes inherit from the built-in `Error` class to communicate domain-specific failure reasons.

## Why does JavaScript have it?
- Network requests fail, invalid user input is supplied, and unexpected edge cases occur. Defensive error handling ensures production reliability and clear debugging traces.

## Basic Syntax
```javascript
try {
    const result = performOperation();
} catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed:", error.message);
} finally {
    cleanupResources(); // Always executes
}

// Custom Error Class
class ValidationError extends Error {
    constructor(message, field) {
        super(message);
        this.name = "ValidationError";
        this.field = field;
    }
}
```

## How it Behaves
1. `throw`: Halts execution of the current function and passes the thrown value to the nearest enclosing `catch` block.
2. `finally`: Executes regardless of whether an error was thrown or caught, making it ideal for closing file descriptors or releasing locks.
3. Standard Error Properties:
   - `error.name`: Name of the error type (e.g. `"TypeError"`, `"ValidationError"`).
   - `error.message`: Human-readable error description.
   - `error.stack`: Stack trace recording execution location.

## Common Mistakes
1. Throwing primitive strings instead of `Error` instances: `throw "failed"` lacks stack trace information. Always use `throw new Error("failed")`.
2. Silent catch blocks: `catch (e) {}` swallows errors and makes debugging impossible. Always log or rethrow if unhandled.
3. Forgetting `super(message)` in custom error constructors: Omitting `super` leaves `error.message` empty and causes missing inheritance setup.

## Small Practical Example
```javascript
function safeJsonParse(rawJson, fallback = null) {
    try {
        return JSON.parse(rawJson);
    } catch {
        return fallback;
    }
}
```

## What to Practice
Implement in `exercise.js`:
